## Cleared but not yet ingested

METR's task-horizon evaluations are the declared primary anchor for the agentic subdomain
from the next chain point. They are not in any released vintage yet, so they carry no row
above. Their basis differs from every row that does: METR's `eval-analysis-public`
repository carries no licence file, and our use rests on **written permission granted to
the AI-Econ Lab on 13 Aug 2026** to derive occupation-year values and redistribute them as
part of this index. A permission granted to us is not a licence that travels with the
data, so when the row is added it must state that basis rather than claim a CC BY
inheritance; DAIOE's own CC BY 4.0 is unaffected, since the released values are our
construction several derivations downstream.

Attribution required wherever that series is redistributed:

> Agentic task-horizon series derived from METR's public evaluation data (metr.org), used
> with permission. METR is not affiliated with this work and does not endorse it.

## Provenance of the benchmark measurements

The frozen 2010--2023 index is built from `measures_metrics_newdata2023.xlsx`, the EFF
AI Progress Measurement data extended through 2023. Measured by the URL each
measurement cites:

| era | rows | recorded from paperswithcode.com |
|---|---|---|
| through 2017 | 1,747 | 21 (1.2%) |
| 2018--2019 | 147 | 60 (40.8%) |
| 2020--2023 | 214 | 109 (50.9%) |
| **total** | **2,108** | **190 (9.0%)** |

Papers with Code is therefore the recorded retrieval point for about half the
measurements in the last four years of the published vintage, and for 9 per cent
overall. It served as a finding aid: each of those rows is a result first published in
a primary paper, and the row names that paper.

`data/derived/pwc_provenance.csv` lists all 190 with their publications. 188 name the
paper, including all 109 dated 2020 or later, and 162 carry a resolved DOI or arXiv
identifier; the remainder carry the title. `retrieved_from` is kept as a record of where
each value was looked up. The frozen workbook itself is unchanged, the sidecar being
generated from it by `scripts/build_pwc_provenance_20260810.py`.

Titles were matched to identifiers automatically, gated on publication year against the
measurement date. The `match_confidence` column describes that match and never the
measurement.

**EFF.** The project's own `LICENSE.md` dual-licenses under CC BY-SA 4.0 and GPLv2+,
and then draws the line we need, verbatim:

> "Much of this Notebook is uncopyrightable data."

The licence covers EFF's written content and source code; the benchmark measurements
are declared uncopyrightable by the source itself, an organisation whose subject is
copyright law. We take the measurements, not the prose and not the notebook code, so
the ShareAlike condition attaches to material we neither use nor redistribute. We
attribute EFF and Felten, Raj and Seamans regardless, as scholarly practice rather than
as a licence condition.

**Papers with Code.** CC BY-SA 4.0, with no equivalent uncopyrightable-data statement.
It is the recorded retrieval point for 190 rows of the frozen source, concentrated in
2018--2023, and it also feeds the 2024 refresh path. Each of those rows is now
attributed to the paper that published the measurement (see above), so the release
carries published facts with their citations rather than the Papers with Code database.

**Direction of travel.** Epoch AI (CC BY 4.0) supplies everything admitted at the 2024
chain point, and the admission rule in DOCUMENTATION.md section 3 requires a
redistribution-clean licence of every series added since.

**The position.** The scores are released CC BY 4.0. The EFF measurements are declared
uncopyrightable by their own source, and the 190 measurements recorded from Papers with
Code are published results attributed to the papers that published them, listed row by
row in `data/derived/pwc_provenance.csv`, rather than an extract of that database.

